This example shows hls::m_axi_buf used with HLS_TOP_AUTORESTART. A single kernel
invocation (launch(1, ...)) re-reads the buffer offset eight times, once per
block of 8192 samples, through read_offset(). A separate updater thread pushes a
new offset with write_offset() while the kernel is running, so offset updates can
land mid-block.

This is an observation test. It never fails. It reports:
  * which region each block latched (taken from its first sample)
  * whether any block mixes samples from more than one region (a torn read)
  * whether the offsets read back match the pushed order

Files Included in this Package
==============================
test.h          Declarations, constants, and the HLS_TOP_AUTORESTART declaration
test.cpp        Design (synthesized)
test_tb.cpp     Testbench, including the updater thread
hls_config.cfg  Vitis HLS configuration
run_hls.tcl     Tcl flow script
run.py           Python flow script (vitis -s run.py)
description.json
README

Requirements
============
hls::m_axi_buf and HLS_TOP_AUTORESTART require a Vitis build that provides them
(2026.2 or later). The default 2026.1 headers do not define m_axi_buf.

Notes
=====
Live behaviour is visible in csim only. The testbench's wall-clock sleeps have no
relationship to RTL simulation time, because cosim captures the testbench stimulus
and replays it. Run cosim only to inspect the ap_none offset register in the
waveform. Cosim is slow: 8192 AXI reads per iteration, 8 iterations.

Running the Design (edit run_hls.tcl to set $hls_exec and enable specific run steps)
=========================================================
vitis-run --mode hls --tcl run_hls.tcl
